WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.670
Local does not automatically mean safe.

2
00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:08.402
A server bound only to loopback is very different from one listening on a LAN interface.

3
00:00:08.402 --> 00:00:19.003
Once a local LLM endpoint is reachable by other devices, it needs firewall rules, access control, logging decisions, and a clear definition of trusted clients.

4
00:00:19.003 --> 00:00:24.500
A load balancer that exposes several backends becomes sensitive infrastructure.

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:35.337
Prompts can contain private documents, source code, operational details, incident notes, personal information, or secrets accidentally pasted into a request.

6
00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:37.378
Outputs can also be sensitive.

7
00:00:37.378 --> 00:00:42.483
Logs may capture bodies, responses, headers, client addresses, and errors.

8
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:49.393
Decide what should be logged, how long logs should remain, and who can read them before expanding access.

9
00:00:49.393 --> 00:00:53.790
Authentication should be considered even in small environments.

10
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:59.837
A lab may use a simple shared token, while a team may need stronger identity-aware controls.

11
00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:05.177
The endpoint should not become a free compute resource for anyone on the network.

12
00:01:05.177 --> 00:01:13.186
Local LLM servers can consume expensive resources, process sensitive information, and interact with connected tools.

13
00:01:13.186 --> 00:01:18.055
TLS, binding addresses, and firewall boundaries should be intentional.

14
00:01:18.055 --> 00:01:25.672
Plain local traffic may be fine on one machine, while LAN traffic may require encryption depending on sensitivity.

15
00:01:25.672 --> 00:01:29.834
Start local-only, then expand access only with a control plan.

16
00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:36.823
A load balancer can centralize security, but a misconfigured gateway can expose every backend behind it.
